                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "4,5/10\nA small but unpolished metroidvania.\n\nThe main issue is the controls. With such minimalist graphics it's amazing how it was possible to fail so greatly at the most important aspect of the game.\nThe gameplay is super rigid for no good reason. The character is slow to attack. The attack animation is also slow. It's so slow that ennemies can kill you during the four sword attacks combo. Each action locks the next. You can't attack and move in the same time. You can't jump and shrink/grow (a power got later) in the air. You can't cancel an attack...\nAnd the developer probably never tried to play his game with a controller. The UP arrow allows you to scroll through the text using the keyboard, and this command is called \"interact\". I tried both the Xbox controller and the Steam Deck and the result is the same: several lines of text disappear in a fraction of second if I use the left joystick. It's as if the game is registering multiple \"UP\" each time the stick is used once.\n\nOdd designs\nThe introduction displays the controls for the player in the background of the first two screens. But why two screens? The first screen display nearly every actions possible and the second screen displays only one command, \"interact\". I was so confused, I believed I had to interact with something on this screen. And I really tried, until I understood there was nothing to do...\nThe health bar is shaped as a heart. Each time an enemy is defeated, it drops hearts. But the hearts don't heal... I had several hundred of them when I've found my first shop. So there is also money to trade. But hearts only exist to be sold for money and that's the only way I found to get money. So why this extra step... It would be simpler if either the shop used hearts as currency, or enemies drop money...\nTo look at the map, it's not just one button. Why complicate things again...\n\nThe narration, while very secondary is also a problem.\nThe introduction is very weird in many ways. A tree is stolen by two dudes. I go after them and when I reach the tree, somehow I lose all the abilities I have. Why? No text, no animation, or nothing else is shown to explain it... Now the tree is speaking and want water. Ok, why not. There are weirdly named bosses that throw 1 or 2 lines of text. Or is it really them? Because every text are displayed in the same place and it's unknown who is speaking. Each time I die, someone speak, but who??? So if I die during a boss fight, text from two different people are mixed in the same place...\n\nBut Grizzland is not irredeemable. The game offers a good dose of exploration, secrets, lore notes, alternative ending, and a clear main objective at each new area: finding water for the tree. The ability to shrink and the ability to pass through walls were also good ideas. And by finding all the hidden notes, it reveals the story of the game, which was quite original.\n\nOverall, it was a clumsy game that did some good things. But my first impression was so bad and the controls were clearly not polished, so I'm not inclined to recommend Grizzland. Yet I took the time to 100% it... So it's a close call.",
